<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1463">1463</a>–<a href="/years/1494">1494</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Pico della <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> (1463–1494) was an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> philosopher, a younger son of the lords of Mirandola. In 1486 he proposed to defend 900 theses in public debate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, introduced by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oration-on-the-dignity-of-man">Oration on the Dignity of Man</a>, but Pope Innocent VIII condemned several of them. He sought to reconcile Platonism, Aristotelianism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the Kabbalah, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, possibly poisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola.jpg" alt="Giovanni Pico della Mirandola" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristofano dell'Altissimo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pico1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giovanni Pico was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, the youngest son of Gianfrancesco I Pico, Lord of Mirandola and Count of Concordia. His mother, Giulia, intended him for the Church, and he was named a papal protonotary, probably an honorary post, at the age of 10. In 1477 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> to study canon law. After his mother died he turned to philosophy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, and from 1480 to 1482 he studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, a major centre of Aristotelianism, where Elia del Medigo taught him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Arabic and translated Jewish manuscripts for him. In 1485 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he probably began the 900 Theses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pico settled for a time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in November 1484, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a>, and Lorenzo protected him until his death in 1492. On his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a love affair in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> led to his being wounded and imprisoned until Lorenzo intervened.</p><p>In Perugia he came to know the Kabbalah and the Hermetic writings, and Rabbi Johannan Alemanno was among his tutors. The most original of his theses concerned the Kabbalah, and he is regarded as the founder of the tradition of Christian Kabbalah. He published the Conclusiones in December 1486 and offered to pay the expenses of scholars who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to debate them.</p><p>In February 1487 Pope Innocent VIII halted the debate and appointed a commission, which condemned thirteen theses. Eventually all 900 were condemned, and the book was the first printed book banned by the Church. Pico fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1488 and was imprisoned at Vincennes until King Charles VIII released him. He lived near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiesole">Fiesole</a> under Lorenzo's protection, and his censures were lifted only in 1493. There he wrote the Heptaplus and De Ente et Uno, and the Disputationes against astrology, which was published after his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Lorenzo's death Pico became a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-savonarola">Girolamo Savonarola</a> and planned to become a monk, but he died in 1494 at the age of 31, on the day Charles VIII entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. A rumour held that his secretary had poisoned him, and Savonarola delivered the funeral oration. In 2007, when the bodies of Pico and the poet Angelo Poliziano were exhumed, forensic tests suggested that both had died of arsenic poisoning. Later investigation found that Pico may have died of acute arsenic poisoning, accidental or deliberate, but that the evidence for Poliziano was weaker. Pico's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oration-on-the-dignity-of-man">Oration on the Dignity of Man</a> has been called the Manifesto of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giovanni Pico della Mirandola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</b>’s Oration on the Dignity of Man, written in 1486 to introduce his 900 theses, celebrated the human freedom to rise or fall by choice; Marsilio Ficino sought to reconcile Platonic philosophy with Christian faith; and Erasmus called his own ideal the “philosophy of Christ”,…</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The humanist <b>Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</b>, author of the Oration on the Dignity of Man, was a younger son of the family.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mirandola">Duchy of Mirandola</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.89,11.07&amp;year=1483" aria-label="Mirandola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Giovanni Pico della Mirandola"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5666 3709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5666 3709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.89,11.07&amp;year=1483">What was here in 1483? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thou, restrained by no narrow bounds, according to thy own free will, in whose power I have placed thee, shalt define thy nature for thyself.&#8221;</p><footer>Oration on the Dignity of Man, 1486 &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1877 &#183; spoken by God to Adam in the Oration &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/41924/pg41924-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have set thee midmost the world, that thence thou mightest the more conveniently survey whatsoever is in the world.&#8221;</p><footer>Oration on the Dignity of Man, 1486 &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1877 &#183; spoken by God to Adam in the Oration &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/41924/pg41924-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thou shalt have power to decline unto the lower or brute creatures. Thou shalt have power to be reborn unto the higher, or divine, according to the sentence of thy intellect.&#8221;</p><footer>Oration on the Dignity of Man, 1486 &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1877 &#183; spoken by God to Adam in the Oration &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/41924/pg41924-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
